\f1\fs22 Hayley introduced herself; Abe the pirate, and Major the fence, reluctantly reciprocated.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 \'93I\'92m wondering if I could ask you a few questions?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The boozers stiffened, their lips thinned.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m with you. I don\'92t think Chanta is guilty of any of the things they accuse him of.\'94
\fs20 \

\fs22 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Just give us five minutes; our show\'92s almost over,\'94 Major said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A huge streaming screen hovered over the blackened cavity where the Stickett Inn had recently existed, still radiating heat and accident insurance pitches. Hayley eyed the program with the kind of distaste reserved for commercial lager. 
\f2\i CSI: Road-Kill County
\f1\i0 , that cop show where every detective was impossibly beautiful, funded by the kind of forensic gravy train no local force could even spell.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The real plot twist, though, was always the same: after striding about with lab coats and glowing tablets, the cops would ignore literally every bit of evidence they\'92d wrangled\'97presumably because they\'92d cocked it all up\'97and nail the murder on whichever unlucky outlier they didn\'92t like from the first act.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You watch,\'94 Abe muttered, not blinking. \'93The six-fingered Indonesian did it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Nah,\'94 said Major, settling in. \'93It\'92s the Estonian with motor neurone disease.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They were both wrong. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ha!\'94 Major said, near-gleeful. \'93They can\'92t even get the cuffs on her!\'94\'85 because the alleged murderer was an octogenarian Peruvian with cerebral palsy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They always arrest the suspect with the least resources to defend themselves,\'94 Hayley muttered bitterly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Abe heard. \'93Yeah, just like real life.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley couldn\'92t entirely refute him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The show flickered to credits. For a fleeting second, all any of them could see was their own reflection in the streaming screen: scruffy, sallow, under-lit by tragedy and the blue-white glow of manufactured justice.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Abe cleared his throat, puffed himself up, and nodded. \'93Ah, well, I\'92m safe. I\'92m independently wealthy.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93So am I,\'94 Major threw in, pouncing on the claim before Hayley could even react.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Abe snorted. \'93You don\'92t even rent your own house!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You call that lean-to of corrugated plastic you have over your head a house?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Abe caught Hayley\'92s eye and, with mock-conspiracy, added, \'93He\'92s just a couch-surfer, Hay\'97Mz Lanning.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Major thumped his pint down, damn near folding one leg of the card table. \'93I\'92ve been at the same address for seventeen years!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Abe leaned in, exaggerating a pitying look. \'93That\'92s how tragic you are\'97couch-surfing at the same place.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Major chewed Abe\'92s contempt for a second. \'93What we both mean to say, Mz Lanning, is we\'92re not beholden to a job to keep a roof overhead.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Someone\'92s roof,\'94 Hayley murmured. She kept a straight face; sometimes conversations like this ran off cliffs you didn\'92t know were there. \'93So, you both have passive incomes?\'94 She had yet to meet a Cloudborne with a \'93passive income\'94 that wasn\'92t laundering or fencing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Abe leaned forward, tapping her wrist with a look that managed to blend both marsupial and lizard discomfort. \'93Never you mind for us. We understand the system.\'94 Wink, nod, flourish.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley suppressed a shudder. She was about to press on, but was interrupted by a voice over the battered P.A. system, echoing out above the street\'92s malaise like a town crier after three gins.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ladies and gentlemen! May I have your attention? Each evening at five-thirty sharp we remember the fallen\'97our beloved Stickett Inn, so violently taken from us in a senseless bombing. Please be upstanding and light your fireworks!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A ragged cheer went up. Abe and Major fumbled for their penny bungers\'97Abe with a grunt, Major with the quick, guilty efficiency of a man who\'92s been cautioned before.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>All up and down the kerb, patrons wobbled to something like attention, clutching packets of Black Cats, Piccolo Petes, Roman Candles, and the odd supermarket fountain that boasted a fizz greater on the packet than in the lighting. There was even some joker with a packet of sparklers taped to his thongs, determined to start a small blaze or a class action.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A foul haze rolled across the bitumen as the first matches struck. Fingers\'97some singed, some trembling with lager or low blood sugar\'97flicked lighters and thumbed cheap fuses. Bungers popped like gunfire from a very small, very underfunded colonial rebellion. String crackers stuttered then fizzled out in puddles of spilt ales and lagers.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A few Roman candles shot sparks sideways into the gutter, prompting a round of half-hearted boos. One of the pub regulars set off a Catherine wheel nailed to an 
\fs20 NO EXIT
\fs22  street sign, which spun for about three seconds before going limp and giving up, to no one\'92s surprise.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Somewhere, a dog went ballistic, and someone shouted \'93Oi!\'94 at nobody, and for one brief, smoky moment, the Stickett Inn lived again, at least in a macabre recreation of its fateful explosion.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley coughed as the air filled with the tang of burnt sulphur, cheap ordnance, and the fried-pork-belly note of nearby skin just slightly too close to the festivities.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>No one called an ambulance. First aid was the last thing anyone wanted, especially in a Cloudborne hospital emergency department.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You know,\'94 Abe said, when the excitement had settled down. \'93We were sitting just in there, maybe two minutes before the bomb went off.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93More like ninety seconds,\'94 Major corrected.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yeah, Chanta had already gone off with some\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You believe a beautiful blonde would waste her time on Chanta?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Blonde?\'94 Hayley prompted, letting the question hang.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yeah,\'94 said Major. \'93She just showed up, introduced herself, and told Chanta they were late for dinner. Then off they went.\'94 Abe looked at Major, his eyes wide as though amazed by his own story. \'93Together.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Major made a sound like a scoff, or a dying kettle. \'93Yeah. Boiled Halo\'92s libido, big time.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Halo. Hayley had been thinking about halos earlier that afternoon, for reasons she still couldn\'92t quite chase down. \'93Did you catch her name?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Major shook his head. \'93She\'92s not Chanta\'92s girlfriend. Way outta his league.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93But not Halo\'92s,\'94 Abe chimed in. \'93He always knew the shortcut to a woman\'92s heart. We used to time him\'97from hello to out the door.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Record\'92s forty-seven seconds,\'94 Major offered, not remotely sheepish.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He was even quicker the night of the bombing.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh yeah. He just went up to some girl and locked lips with her. Probably thinking about the blonde that collected Chanta.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley steadied herself and swiped open her tablet, thumb dancing, presenting a picture. \'93Is this her?\'94 She held up Sarah Jacobson\'92s profile.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They leaned in.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yep,\'94 Abe confirmed. \'93Uhuh,\'94 echoed Major.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley exhaled, then forced herself to look up through her lashes.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You know her?\'94 Major pressed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ah, no\'85\'94 Hayley said, hedging awkwardness.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Hey, is she involved in framing Chanta for all this terrorism nonsense?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Um, no\'85 she\'92s\'97 Tell me about Halo,\'94 Hayley said, keen on redirecting. \'93He\'92s a friend?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93More Chanta\'92s friend,\'94 Abe said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We called him the bodyguard.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Why?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Always getting Chanta outta scrapes. Especially after a few too many.\'94 Major mimed a drink.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Chanta can be an angry drunk,\'94 Abe confided; Major gave him a warning glare.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You know.\'94 Major\'92s eyes ticked up to the Layer\'92s cloudy brooding. \'93Can\'92t remember the last time Chanta was anywhere without Halo shadowing him.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Now they\'92re both gone gone.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, missing, not gone.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Halo\'92s missing too?\'94 Hayley checked. \'93He wasn\'92t caught in the blast?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Abe gave a snort. \'93Halo? No, he\'92s more likely to set a bomb than get blown up by one.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley\'92s attention sharpened. \'93Are you saying he\'92s the bomber?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What? No, just that nothing bad sticks to Halo. Always calm. Always in control.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He hated Chanta making off with the blonde.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Called her a stuffy bitch.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93
\f2\i Stacy
\f1\i0  bitch,\'94 Major corrected.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yeah, whatever\'97a real object of desire.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Unusual for Halo to miss out,\'94 Major added, like it was tragic history.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley\'92s mouth twisted. \'93No guy always gets the girl,\'94 she said, but didn\'92t say the rest: not unless there\'92s coercion.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Abe snorted. \'93No one believes us when we talk up Halo.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That\'92s why we take pictures,\'94 said Major.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You what?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Pictures\'97of Halo, on the dance floor, leading them out. We get free drinks from the stories alone.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Proof\'92s needed,\'94 Abe said. \'93People think we\'92re full of it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Missed our shot the night of the bombing, though. He was out the door that quick, all we could do was chase him.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That\'92s what saved us. We\'92d have copped the bomb sitting at our table. But we had to get the Halo money shot.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Didn\'92t get it, obviously. Bomb went up just as we left by the side door.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yeah, Halo always likes a discreet exit.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A silence dropped for eight or ten seconds, the sort that never lasts long enough.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley\'92s thoughts split: one thing she clearly wanted, another she dreaded she should want.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Look, I\'92m with you,\'94 she said as off-handed as she could manage. \'93As I said, I think Chanta\'92s innocent of all this.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Too right,\'94 Abe said. \'93You might check out that Sarah, though. Too good to be true, and so on.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley managed a smile and stood, as if ready to leave. \'93Could you give me Chanta\'92s address?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Major sat back, putting on a superior look. \'93Can\'92t you find that in your data swamps?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley bit back the news: Chantara\'92s swamp had run dry. Too many odd features to this case\'97no need to spook the witnesses yet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93If it\'92s okay, I\'92d like to follow up quickly. Chanta\'92s missing.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sure, love,\'94 Major said, with only a moderate dose of condescension. He tapped her a contact card.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Thank you,\'94 Hayley said. She turned to go, paused\'97timing the moment, then swung back. \'93It all sounds like a fantasy, really. Like something medieval.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The man always in charge?\'94 Major teased.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley huffed, feigning relief. \'93Ah, you got me. I supposed you think I\'92m into romance novels?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Abe perked up. \'93Pirate romances, eh?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley nodded, with just enough self-consciousness.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93But it\'92s silly, isn\'92t it?\'94 she pressed. \'93One man with all that power?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We\'92ve seen proof.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92d like\'97\'94 Hayley started, left the line dangling.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Abe\'92s patience snapped. \'93To see the proof?\'94 He handed over his tablet, auto-scrolling.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley widened her eyes, played up the shock (then a hint of fascination). Abe and Major relaxed, draining their beers, and missed Hayley flicking the whole photo roll to her own tablet with one discreet tap.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Thank you,\'94 she said, and vanished.\
